#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e07bdf4f-6187-5516-b017-6bb33da91de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/ebd57b72a8e825778bce33f84bcbec2a0bd8d852a5b2ce25a707224b39d07f99.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325705} {:answer/id #uuid "b35691b3-c33a-57a7-9c1f-b43026489905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325706} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8b8a81-5f08-5d6b-8679-b5c9663abb68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/0d4597773001223e64f19e1db23c441cc7d4ffb1481c564b63f456fa05e4a0ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325707} {:answer/id #uuid "d76c1d48-acd7-5ac5-9e3b-8f58292addf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/33c6eba539c7bc77e4c349c444da281fb3945aeb9f48018986e8c6373bc0e3b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325708} {:answer/id #uuid "8059b462-6b05-5b2e-b2c5-aef0c7b76978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8059b462-6b05-5b2e-b2c5-aef0c7b76978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325709} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ff8b86-ea5e-533e-9e3a-2e68d8c8e7a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baf40def-809e-5a8c-bd6e-f8be8448a5df" :question/math-academy-id "q-177649" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab251298-825c-5651-9c5b-498e30a84797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/98077ef737a2487f9e480b7ffd42376785c5681e2651b98e7b71abb4e645d876.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325712} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b9de57-7448-5069-bc6d-824054bab476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325713} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee24989-0864-55f6-a02d-4890b410f519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/749f4fb3a6416147cdd69c08bf806c28e2112929453bb8ae405c48452d82126b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325714} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd0815e-1533-5591-8a51-8db7447b6810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/aaa04c2578557d03dd144c27e6a5c223126830ba2a3f8288adc9239daf604e55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325715} {:answer/id #uuid "bf04a8dc-3ccb-56f6-881e-bda5ca60a731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/36b36ac05cb1054e198f2e40a2d0ffdd40e952abaa8ddf5c946a38fde946adc8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9b9de57-7448-5069-bc6d-824054bab476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325713} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eab96d1-a181-526e-a01d-0369f3b13504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62556693-fd48-5895-851c-d4aab5200ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-177650" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/7587daeaea45c5ef3449ecb676133c60bbedb7819308783bac835f3ae9c6883c.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {i}={(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/b926204a3d58b25889f1c79dd0f65c1ea990c67bc88e869004396a77970e8dd4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8193065-f912-54e3-a5a9-813626f9b605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325719} {:answer/id #uuid "78eba5cc-34ee-5edd-9210-d85f1bf9f5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/9303385b85b1bedfa6add9933b1953a0cdc74581c0f69ffed4d4ad3f0a113bf1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325720} {:answer/id #uuid "197b155a-1137-5fd8-a08b-e04a2301cda0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325721} {:answer/id #uuid "4720e5b1-b1e3-51f2-9a6b-cc14c94fe161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/146139f4429b4b864a027a263abe7a6133b5b96c48aae37d5f9bc1a3635263ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325722} {:answer/id #uuid "64a6f7e5-cb42-5969-a030-23576a1511f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64a6f7e5-cb42-5969-a030-23576a1511f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325723} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6d0ccc-c30d-50da-8f69-63f8dff8461c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bfdf4e4-11aa-513b-b13d-ea378cad8242" :question/math-academy-id "q-181779" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/067b3b386273b09d5614f934f6525bd3e43ff46f420eb8aa7e744f635ff6d101.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a870f65350ad00d258471079a34bec49a47d331c176dc7a91b3c7aae5ae968dd.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed9ee614-2ae0-58f1-9711-5f7bc6898e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/3ea69cc945f29707432415ed194c8abdec6b60056c8c64c845f27b3139cbd3d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325726} {:answer/id #uuid "4945781f-823f-51d3-aeba-1a167b61b3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/e3550a78d58d91efb3eb012cfad691ee22b7bd216de7910850a289db3ab9e2b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325727} {:answer/id #uuid "13d3fbbd-c256-54b5-b6f9-f1d8105450d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325728} {:answer/id #uuid "a82ee82e-a95e-5662-be50-284d02d58e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/35b2f7b26a964f8892ba85bcdeb4fae5312d515044e355cc6a1f989efc8a825c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325729} {:answer/id #uuid "41a41125-ca21-5bdd-930f-13a3e4b81176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a41125-ca21-5bdd-930f-13a3e4b81176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325730} :answer-field/id #uuid "49212bd4-a60b-5884-95a7-97caedcf98d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "532e58fd-76c5-5142-8167-f5bc2c3677bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-181979" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ce5ed93-e51f-5fb4-9655-57dc44837b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181984-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195455} {:answer/id #uuid "a0df5145-6a0c-585d-b61c-ec795eee233e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181984-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195456} {:answer/id #uuid "bb31eead-cabc-512e-9d51-5649a1d7ce5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181984-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195457} {:answer/id #uuid "33bcd099-b438-5d84-aa62-feee4347e931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181984-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195458} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccd8a29-42a8-5a0d-9f81-51a7c4a50c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181984-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb31eead-cabc-512e-9d51-5649a1d7ce5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181984-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195457} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c387200-f91e-5830-b073-a434b0094c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10624e27-fb3c-5852-bfb2-5c112258247b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181984" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the set $S=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,1],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/494c9d77b0ff339b90792270c9d641054a03a2918e3c56a93747b42a77ee3ccf.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,1]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/7c5ac5dad0aaacb4347c260d634e6bcd2d6102b25d51813fe106803417cc7539.png)"}]}
